 :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-274700/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "2cc0ce40-0864-5a33-a09f-3e7a2e290b53" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-274700/field-4/7ef186fd8b5a1c52c90d35337c0050c3c2cd876fe199bd9866d5167b1165edd0" :answer-field/choices ["q-274700/field-4/7ef186fd8b5a1c52c90d35337c0050c3c2cd876fe199bd9866d5167b1165edd0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-274700/field-5/3202c38f69df9ba7e6146205db4b06907313ea52812cc80593d5589029147966" :answer/id #uuid "41a10b61-200f-5d2f-8f1e-41e9abc92fee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{19\\pi}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-274700/field-5" :answer-field/id #uuid "0e741ea3-e2d2-58e6-9ad3-9c08f58597ad" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-274700/field-5/3202c38f69df9ba7e6146205db4b06907313ea52812cc80593d5589029147966" :answer-field/choices ["q-274700/field-5/3202c38f69df9ba7e6146205db4b06907313ea52812cc80593d5589029147966"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-274700/field-6/9c85ea125e56a76fbf756f5eedac6011c7785e0ad22a50a8a43d0b1bae0512b2" :answer/id #uuid "2918c7e9-be4f-50fc-9143-98598cd05233" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{23\\pi}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-274700/field-6" :answer-field/id #uuid "dd3cf5f0-4488-5c51-b391-aa950d38f750" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-274700/field-6/9c85ea125e56a76fbf756f5eedac6011c7785e0ad22a50a8a43d0b1bae0512b2" :answer-field/choices ["q-274700/field-6/9c85ea125e56a76fbf756f5eedac6011c7785e0ad22a50a8a43d0b1bae0512b2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-274700" :question/id #uuid "8191d21e-e7b2-58ac-88d7-73913c678fbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-274700" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the **exact solutions** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)=-1,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\,{x}_{3}=$ \n{{field-3}}$,\\,\\,{x}_{4}=$ \n{{field-4}}$,\\,\\,{x}_{5}=$ \n{{field-5}}$,\\,\\,{x}_{6}=$ \n{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(4n-1)\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{11\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=4$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$\n\n- Setting $n=5$ gives $x=\\frac{19\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=6$ gives $x=\\frac{23\\pi }{12}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ \n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{7\\pi }{12},\\,\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{11\\pi }{12},\\,\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\,{x}_{5}=\\frac{19\\pi }{12},\\,\\,{x}_{6}=\\frac{23\\pi }{12}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-274700/field-1" "q-274700/field-2" "q-274700/field-3" "q-274700/field-4" "q-274700/field-5" "q-274700/field-6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f40f50e-49a7-5eda-b642-dc7085569b23"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-274700"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-8558/selection/5c9afa5cc2a010d299f64108d858e47b80c78fd7d38219f2e6390e2bd4607bc6" :answer/id #uuid "ab28603e-6e91-573d-a88c-c9e594b82560" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-8558/selection/0dd6da98400cc89eac36ae79dcd4c4c8e9cf7e1ffcb58b5827820d38e236f99c" :answer/id #uuid "00ccd903-5c24-528a-8d48-63546a00977a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-8558/selection/c2db034559420b121b995a068bdfea5fe16f2b700d9ebe187704e3ea55c1239e" :answer/id #uuid "a1d74da3-5418-53e7-a8a4-5d086c60c804" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-8558/selection/4a1f80a5e015d55bc5fc30584b7cda177f967bf1ecb546be292320b37e6a91db" :answer/id #uuid "f55bf7fb-6cdd-5bb7-8d02-fa0a1b30f7b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-8558/selection/26734acc4349599b013ff5890611204a590e31e398b69a9ba8c4c553e3de6e98" :answer/id #uuid "1fd32d65-a4a8-5abf-af77-2ed08981df9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-8558/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8d04fa4-e2a1-588e-a8ca-2887dac48ff9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-8558/selection/26734acc4349599b013ff5890611204a590e31e398b69a9ba8c4c553e3de6e98" :answer-field/choices ["q-8558/selection/5c9afa5cc2a010d299f64108d858e47b80c78fd7d38219f2e6390e2bd4607bc6" "q-8558/selection/0dd6da98400cc89eac36ae79dcd4c4c8e9cf7e1ffcb58b5827820d38e236f99c" "q-8558/selection/c2db034559420b121b995a068bdfea5fe16f2b700d9ebe187704e3ea55c1239e" "q-8558/selection/4a1f80a5e015d55bc5fc30584b7cda177f967bf1ecb546be292320b37e6a91db" "q-8558/selection/26734acc4349599b013ff5890611204a590e31e398b69a9ba8c4c553e3de6e98"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-8558" :question/id #uuid "b2133d03-8737-5ad8-a61a-2ae63b6db3e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-8558" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{7\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{7\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{7\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{13\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{13\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(6n-13)\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=4$ gives $x=\\frac{11\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=5$ gives $x=\\frac{17\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=6$ gives $x=\\frac{23\\pi }{12}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5\\pi }{12},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{12},\\,\\frac{17\\pi }{12},\\,\\frac{23\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ and ${x}_{4}=\\frac{23\\pi }{12},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}+\\frac{23\\pi }{12}=\\frac{28\\pi }{12}=\\frac{7\\pi }{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-8558/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3bd5e5c-b8d1-53e5-9968-8bef8faceb4b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-8558"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124345/selection/94786ff5da18eca7b34968478ef0ca21ee840249e1ca4e89186df64931ae3be0" :answer/id #uuid "8f32aee0-2a31-5231-875c-eda816c432d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124345/selection/e5c63bc832c49e18fb2678001d953240c26767b2f942793f012ef9f6442d3680" :answer/id #uuid "a4d3c97c-13bb-5bec-a61f-3a5788ffeb9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124345/selection/eb812f27219f6aa58813c9c74c442c8eeae741dad3c1dfa26acafe737dbee5f4" :answer/id #uuid "31784df0-4201-5545-a910-2a76cd6fe250" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124345/selection/49038b61ea067d6dd93788348f33b8daf64ac00ab39a6be298018d209f477567" :answer/id #uuid "90da44e1-f12b-5dfd-a0cb-0a1ca57f017b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124345/selection/c2db034559420b121b995a068bdfea5fe16f2b700d9ebe187704e3ea55c1239e" :answer/id #uuid "7533ba62-b7b6-54cb-b91c-e375dea2d9c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124345/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd95be2f-782d-5c9d-b699-586d1c627f61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124345/selection/94786ff5da18eca7b34968478ef0ca21ee840249e1ca4e89186df64931ae3be0" :answer-field/choices ["q-124345/selection/94786ff5da18eca7b34968478ef0ca21ee840249e1ca4e89186df64931ae3be0" "q-124345/selection/e5c63bc832c49e18fb2678001d953240c26767b2f942793f012ef9f6442d3680" "q-124345/selection/eb812f27219f6aa58813c9c74c442c8eeae741dad3c1dfa26acafe737dbee5f4" "q-124345/selection/49038b61ea067d6dd93788348f33b8daf64ac00ab39a6be298018d209f477567" "q-124345/selection/c2db034559420b121b995a068bdfea5fe16f2b700d9ebe187704e3ea55c1239e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124345" :question/id #uuid "2a2e563d-e079-592c-b11c-ba8b756c914d" :question/math-academy-id "q-124345" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\sqrt{3}=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(1+6n)\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{13\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{19\\pi }{12}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{7\\pi }{12},\\frac{13\\pi }{12},\\frac{19\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ and ${x}_{4}=\\frac{19\\pi }{12},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{19\\pi }{12}=\\frac{20\\pi }{12}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-124345/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3bd5e5c-b8d1-53e5-9968-8bef8faceb4b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124345"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-328359/field-1/438469ab2d71e1bb8b63f3164bacb1bbb54e29062b14dce6a666b63ac0081609" :answer/id #uuid "07eb5011-0b61-5397-8672-d