Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/assets/385eda9503b6359ea4dde248d5bbcd7234850cbeb08ac88a58c24f9d4dc6a68c.png)\n\n- Setting ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/assets/96339988564b1da5684054323ea954614e0e35690e7aeb3b47aad66938c1cd11.png) gives ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/assets/21c1fdff5197b7a38094849a957fc032e55b39c064066715b2c134f93fede9e3.png)\n\n- Setting ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/assets/b3ed24ffd49ecbb6d34459118c56c6426d20d7668736aec9217a606c95d50734.png) gives ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/assets/077113b2a8efeadc197b3327b996b0052ea1d55829c778333b97e55bddb48123.png)\n\n- Setting ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/assets/012cd6305c9a8684e431a0e86bae0bfb4c82cff904f4f443d43e54ebc87276b3.png) gives ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/assets/c1259a1eb248107a531d060a9c19e4cffdc3ecbce921a659073b872f1d827976.png)\n\nAll other values of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/assets/1c02b6881da53ff1f7bc8f4eabd52747f37a9779dcd1c36e216c6722a52dfd90.png) generate solutions outside the domain ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/assets/0b64f87945ae9eb945a9a840a7006eebd42679f9dc724cbadc82b38f59277bf8.png) So, the solutions ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/assets/f6fb2411413354f3d0f3cc601a735455b9e5bb134675c420bf171fd648633565.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/assets/7ce9161c04b1e085cdd58f5f3588db7aec98df7911e5f8e7f64d5858b8842ea2.png) (written in ascending order) are\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/assets/1c12090770fea55e112497cab483d909e97bb9cc1bc5fff3a18e47566d8fbf7b.png)\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/assets/8855dc6d07396778d42c239b5594938440375067d54743975ccc2c6050409d6f.png) is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979276/assets/a26ed6e8cee8da6886d11ee5d9a5d1bbdc56602b01b7df20b675895fcc675d16.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3789"] :question/problem "Solve the equation $2\\sqrt{3}\\tan\\left(2x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) - 2 = 0$ for $0\\leq x < 2\\pi.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})-2 & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(6n-1)\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2,\\ldots$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0 \\leq x \\lt 2\\pi.$ Notice that setting $n=0$ gives a result outside of the domain.\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{12}.$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\dfrac{11\\pi}{12}.$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\dfrac{17\\pi}{12}.$\n\n- Setting $n=4$ gives $x=\\dfrac{23\\pi}{12}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0 \\leq x \\lt 2\\pi.$ So, the solutions $x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{12}, \\dfrac{11\\pi}{12}, \\dfrac{17\\pi}{12}, \\dfrac{23\\pi}{12} .\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3794"] :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\tan\\left(3x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 1$ for $-\\pi < x \\leq \\pi.$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2,\\ldots,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi < x \\leq \\pi.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=0.$\n\n- Setting $n=\\pm1$ gives $x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\n- Setting $n=\\pm2$ gives $x=\\pm\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\pi.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi < x \\leq \\pi.$ So, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx = 0,\\, \\pm \\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\, \\pm\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}, \\, \\pi.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
